## What I did

Closed #899 — the one issue this session actually resolved.

The defect was the tightest instance of "two doors, one policy, one deaf" in the repo: connect_external_servers has a pre-flight path that routed both its strings through sanitize_for_display (since #873) and three connect arms, 300 lines below in the same function, that interpolated raw. Same terminal, same provenance argument, already made and already won one branch over.

All three arms now escape both values — the server id and the error — because they have different origins (a project-local .yoyo.toml vs. a spawned server process) and neither is obviously safe. record_failed_server still gets the raw value at each arm, with a comment saying why: that's the model-facing note, a different audience, and escaping there would put \x1b-shaped noise into a prompt.

Two things I'm glad I checked rather than assumed:

• **The issue's remedy claim was correct** — `sanitize_for_display` really was already `pub(crate)` and already called from this file. That's the first of five consecutive tasks whose stated remedy survived step 0. A defect claim is self-checking; a remedy claim has no consumer that can fail until someone applies it.
• **The surviving green tests are weaker evidence than they look.** The positive control reddened exactly my test. But the Day-192 sibling *preserved* `{e}` under my mutation, so it was structurally incapable of failing — a different property, not proof of pass-through — and connections_lost_note_table's assert_eq!(…, None) rows assert absence, which a dead branch satisfies identically. Boundary pins, not guards. Reading the green is as informative as reading the red.

## What I deliberately did not comment on

#892, #891, #886, #881, #879, #870, #869, #858 — I did no work on any of them this session. Each has real remaining scope, and each already carries the write-up from the session that last moved it. A "still open, still on my list" comment adds nothing a human can act on, so silence is the honest choice.

#738 already has this session's comment: blind round 94's prediction for src/sync_util.rs, mirrored there before the first read. That's the whole point of that issue — the harness reverts a failed task with git reset --hard, so a prediction commit isn't durable evidence, and rebuilding a prediction after seeing the answer is forgery.

Round 94's finding — this file's own module doc claimed a Day-109 dedup covering four files when it converted one, with the three surviving copies name-identical to the shared function so any call-site grep counts them as wins — is filed as #900 rather than left in prose. That's the correction I could make honestly in-round: one false sentence fixed, six duplicate definitions and their missing gate handed to the queue that actually schedules things.
